ertarian economists  like von Mises and Hayek  and to say that our society  spontaneously generates peace prosperity  the fruit of conscious action but not of  conscious design and I'm wondering but  what is the chief benefit then of  getting beyond copyright well I'm glad  you brought that up it's kind of an  abstract point but I think it'll  resonate with libertarians I analogize  the common law system to the  packet-switching that makes the internet  work so well and the same analogy  extended to copyright makes copyright  look like a circuit switching kind of  the old phone network now there's  advantages to bow  but I think too many people don't  appreciate sufficiently how the common  law can generate these wonderful things  unforeseeable things really things that  are hard for us to predict because it's  a more free flexible system so I think a  lot of the boons we get from copyright  we could get from the common law as well  we could have new works of art and the  dissemination of books and photos and  things if we didn't have copyright sort  of getting in the way it's a top-down  process comes out of Washington suffers  all the legislative failures that  legislation all these spaces and that's  in contrast to this bottom-up process a  spontaneous order we get from the common  law so insofar as possible I think we  would benefit from exiting that  statutory regime very controlled and  rigid and top-down towards a more  flexible open spontaneous system we'd  get with the common law you mentioned  that there are certain industries that  really aren't don't have any copyright  protection whatsoever like fashion for  example what would happen if we got rid  of it entirely I don't think anybody  knows for certain but I do think it's  instructive that perfumes jokes recipes  furniture design automobile designs the  list goes on and on none of those  industries have copyright protection and  there's no shortage of innovation I mean  do you lack for new perfumes I don't  know what to do with all the perfumes so  there's ways people find to make money  off their creativity in the absence of  copyright and maybe that would be true  in other areas as well if we gave it a  try but I'm not calling for abolishing  copyright because really I don't think  anybody can know it's more subtle than  that what I proposed I think we should  set up the system so that innovators  have an opportunity and an incentive to  try standing on their own feet as it  were in the common law system to maybe  once in awhile give up some of their  copyright privileges and try just using  the common law maybe we'd end up with  something even for books and photos like  what we see with regard to jokes and  recipes and perfumes so the book talks  about that it's a tricky thing to  implement but I do think that we could  do with less copyright I think it's  worth a try I don't want to strike opera  from the books I think it'd be a mistake  but I do would like to see reforms  towards the last copyright I wonder if  you could talk a little bit about why  you're here in DC Ben  on Capitol Hill for a while and what has  been your experience actually getting in  with legislators well you know it's  interesting if you had asked me a  question like that five years ago I  think I would have said oh it's gonna be  more of the same more copyright more  copyright more copyright because back  then there was just one lobby basically  it was you know the publisher is  Hollywood and there wasn't much on the  other side so politically we're  interesting and they were entering an  interesting area because of a couple  things one some of the tech giants in  Silicon Valley see the copyright gets in  their way you think of some body like  Google for example they're not always  big fans of copyright it prevents them  from making money and on the other side  we of that on that side of the fence  also we see the public finally starting  to open its eyes SOPA PIPA that debacle  burned a lot of legislators and they do  not want to face another SOPA PIPA that  was for them a real kick in the pants  they realized oh my gosh you know your  average everyday voter now realizes  copyright does influence them what could  Pete was putting up these Plata's send  an email to your representative if you  don't like this bill and man they got a  lot of email so now legislators are  finally starting to see that when they  ramp up copyright they also can  aggravate a lot of people there's two  sides to the debate now so there's hope  that at least the next time they touch  the Copyright Act it won't be just you  know tightening up the screws a little  more was your message well-received I  think that lawmakers are interested in  hearing other perspectives to their  credit you know I less what lawmakers  are supposed to do what comes out of  legislative process who knows but I  think more so than in recent history  they're willing to consider other  approaches to copyright they too have  the internet on their desks and I'm sure  they saw those black pages on Wikipedia  and so there's hope that we'll get a  more balanced approach to copyright in  future years all right and we'll leave  it there Tom Bell thank you very much  for talking with us Thank You Todd I'm  Todd crane in for a reason TV

